To apply a Gaussian filter before applying the Sobel operator, you can use the `fsme` function in MATLAB. Here's the modified code:

```matlab
% Read input image
input_image = imread('image.jpg');

% Convert to grayscale
gray_image = rgb2gray(input_image);

% Apply Gaussian filter
filtered_image = fsme(gray_image, [3 3], 'gaussian');

% Define Sobel operator in x direction
sobel_x = [-1 0 1; -2 0 2; -1 0 1];

% Define Sobel operator in y direction
sobel_y = [-1 -2 -1; 0 0 0; 1 2 1];

% Apply Sobel operator in x direction
edge_image_x = conv2(filtered_image, sobel_x, 'same');

% Apply Sobel operator in y direction
edge_image_y = conv2(filtered_image, sobel_y, 'same');

% Combine edges in both directions
edge_image = sqrt(edge_image_x.^2 + edge_image_y.^2);

% Display edge image
imshow(edge_image);
```

In this code, we first apply a Gaussian filter to the gray image using the `fsme` function. The `[3 3]` argument specifies the size of the Gaussian kernel, and the 'gaussian' string specifies the type of filter.

After applying the Gaussian filter, we define the Sobel operators in x and y directions as before, and apply them to the filtered image using the `conv2` function. Finally, we combine the edges in both directions and display the result using the `imshow` function.

This modified code will reduce the noise in the image by applying a Gaussian filter before applying the Sobel operator.

