To fix the CORS error in your WordPress site when using Next.js, you can follow these steps:

1. Install and configure the `cors` package in your Next.js project. You can do this by running the following command in your terminal:
```bash
npm install cors
```
Then, add the following code to your `next.config.js` file:
```javascript
const withCors = require('cors')({ options: { origin: 'https://your-wordpress-site.com' } });

module.exports = withCors({
  // Your other Next.js configuration options
});
```
Replace `https://your-wordpress-site.com` with the URL of your WordPress site. This will allow requests from your WordPress site to be made to your Next.js API routes.

2. In your WordPress site, add the following code to your `wp-config.php` file:
```php
add_filter('allowed_http_origins', function ($origins) {
    $origins[] = 'https://your-nextjs-site.com';
    return $origins;
});
```
Replace `https://your-nextjs-site.com` with the URL of your Next.js site. This will allow requests from your Next.js site to be made to your WordPress site.

3. Finally, make sure that you have set up the correct authentication credentials for your WordPress site in your Next.js API routes. You can do this by using the `wp_set_auth_cookie()` function in your WordPress site and then verifying the authentication credentials in your Next.js API routes using the `is_user_logged_in()` function.

After following these steps, you should be able to resolve the CORS error in your WordPress site when using Next.js.

