Here's a decorator for Nest.js middleware that checks the NODE_ENV variable and returns true or false based on its value:

```typescript
import { Injectable, CanActivate } from '@nestjs/common';

@Injectable()
export class EnvironmentMiddleware implements CanActivate {
  canActivate(context) {
    const nodeEnv = process.env.NODE_ENV;

    if (nodeEnv === 'true') {
      return true;
    } else if (nodeEnv === 'false') {
      return false;
    } else {
      throw new Error('Invalid NODE_ENV value');
    }
  }
}
```

To use this middleware, you can add it to your controller's `use` property like this:

```typescript
import { Controller, Get } from '@nestjs/common';
import { EnvironmentMiddleware } from './environment.middleware';

@Controller('your-controller-path')
export class YourController {
  constructor(private readonly environmentMiddleware: EnvironmentMiddleware) {}

  @Get()
  async getData() {
    await this.environmentMiddleware.canActivate(this);
    // Your code here
  }
}
```

This middleware will check the NODE_ENV variable and return true if it's equal to 'true', false if it's equal to 'false', and throw an error if it's not a valid value. You can adjust the logic inside the `canActivate` method to suit your needs.

