  // Get the URL of the current tab
  var url = tabs[0].url;

  // Check if the URL is allowed to be injected with our script
  if (isAllowedUrl(url)) {
    // Inject the script into the current tab
    chrome.tabs.executeScript(tabs[0].id, {file: "your_script.js"});
  } else {
    // Display an error message if the URL is not allowed
    console.error("The current URL is not allowed to be injected with our script.");
  }
});
```
Replace `your_script.js` with the name of your JavaScript file that contains the code you want to inject into the target webpage.
3. In your manifest file (manifest.json), add the following permissions to allow your extension to inject scripts into webpages:
```json
"permissions": ["activeTab", "tabs"]
```
4. Save both files and reload your Chrome extension by clicking on the extension icon in the toolbar or going to `chrome://extensions/`.
5. Now, when you visit a webpage that matches the allowed URL pattern, your JavaScript file will be injected into the page.

