Here's a PowerShell script that recursively checks folders for SIDs that occur only in the SIDHistory of any user or group in the domain:

```powershell
# Define the function to check a folder and its subfolders
function CheckFolder($folderPath) {
    $acl = Get-Acl -Path $folderPath
    foreach ($access in $acl.Access) {
        if ($access.IdentityReference.Value -eq "S-1-5-21-XXXX-YYYY-ZZZZ") {
            # Check if the SID exists in the SIDHistory of any user or group in the domain
            $domain = (Get-ADDomain).Name
            $searchBase = "DC=$domain,DC=local"
            $filter = "(objectClass=user)(sid=$access.IdentityReference.Value)"
            $users = Get-ADUser -Filter $filter -SearchBase $searchBase
            if ($users.Count -eq 0) {
                Write-Host "The SID $access.IdentityReference.Value does not exist in the SIDHistory of any user or group in the domain."
            }
        }
    }
    foreach ($subFolder in (Get-ChildItem -Path $folderPath -Directory)) {
        CheckFolder($subFolder.FullName)
    }
}

# Define the root folder to start the search
$rootFolder = "C:\YourRootFolder"

# Check the root folder and its subfolders
CheckFolder($rootFolder)
```

Replace `C:\YourRootFolder` with the root folder you want to start the search from. This script will recursively check all folders within the specified root folder and its subfolders for SIDs that occur only in the SIDHistory of any user or group in the domain. If an SID is found that does not exist in the SIDHistory of any user or group in the domain, the script will display a message indicating so.
